<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sicca Veneria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sicca, El Kef</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sicca Veneria was an ancient town in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, on the site of modern El Kef, named after its sanctuary of a goddess identified with Venus. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> it was where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> gathered before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary-war">Mercenary War</a> of 241 BCE, and it later belonged to the Numidian kingdom. It became a Roman colony under Augustus and an important town of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sicca-veneria.jpg" alt="Sicca Veneria" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Moumou82 at French Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Kef.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sicca Veneria stood 175 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> and some 40 kilometres east of the present border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. Its site is the cliff face of Jebel Dyr, a table-topped mountain, at about 780 metres, the highest elevation of any city in Tunisia. From antiquity the town was the principal city of the High-Tell Mountains and of the north-west of the country, and until recent times it was the political centre, the most important religious centre and the dominant stronghold of that region. The great mesa known as the Jugurtha Tableland can be seen from it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The character of the worship of Venus at the Roman colony suggests that it began as a Phoenician settlement. It was later a Numidian stronghold, and under the Caesars it became a fashionable residential city and one of the chief centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. The Christian apologist Arnobius the Elder, who died in 330, lived and taught here, and the early Christian writer Lactantius, who lived from about 250 to 325, is also associated with the town.</p><p>In the early 5th century a priest of Sicca named Apiarius provoked a dispute between the churches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> over the jurisdiction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, when he appealed to Rome against his excommunication by Carthage. In 499 Fulgentius of Ruspe was ordered to be scourged here by an Arian priest.</p><p>Around 439 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> conquered the African Romans near the coast, and the town eventually became part of the Berber Kingdom of Dorsale. It was raided in 688 during the Umayyad conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. The name El Kef has been used since the 16th century. A kasbah to house a permanent garrison was built in the 17th century and fortified ramparts were added in 1740, yet Algerian forces still took and pillaged the city in 1756, and French troops occupied it from 1881.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The modern city of El Kef is the capital of the Kef Governorate and had a population of 45,191 at the 2004 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Roman remains include fragments of a large temple dedicated to Hercules, baths, ancient cisterns and the ruins of a Christian basilica whose apse is intact. A well preserved three-aisled basilica of the beginning of the 5th century, named Dar El Kous and dedicated to Saint Peter, has also been found. The city is a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> devotion, with the Sidi Bou Makhlouf Mausoleum, and it was the provisional capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “El Kef” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
